+## Summary
+
+1. src/flask/blueprints.py — In Blueprint.__init__, after super().__init__(...) and before assigning self.name, raise ValueError if name contains a dot. Added a versionchanged note in the class docstring.
+
+2. tests/test_blueprints.py — Removed test_dotted_names (it created blueprints named "myapp.frontend"/"myapp.backend", now invalid) and added test_dotted_name_not_allowed, which asserts that flask.Blueprint("app.ui", __name__) raises ValueError. Kept test_dotted_names_from_app since it uses a non-dotted name.
+
+## Why
+
+Nested blueprints use "." as the separator between a parent blueprint name and its children (see the name_prefix construction and the "."-joined endpoint/error-handler keys in register). A dot inside a blueprint name is ambiguous with a nesting boundary and can cause endpoint collisions and incorrect url_for resolution. Flask already rejects dots in endpoint names; this adds the analogous guard for blueprint names at construction time, with a clear ValueError.
+
+## Tests
+
+Run: pytest tests/test_blueprints.py
+
+The new test_dotted_name_not_allowed should pass, and the rest of the suite (including test_dotted_names_from_app and test_nested_blueprint) should continue to pass. The removed test_dotted_names no longer applies because the feature it relied on — dots in blueprint names — is now explicitly disallowed.
